\chapter{Kernel manifests, plans, and CDD metadata}
\label{ch:93}
\index{Kernel manifests, plans, and CDD metadata}
\index{Kernels!manifest}

The MuLIB manifest records headers, manuals, dependencies, fixtures and implementation kinds. Planners combine those records with explicitly registered recipes, including support sources under \OnxCodeTerm{library/}. The difference between a dependency include and an implementation object matters when assembling a single kernel translation unit. CDD records describe capabilities and typed declarations, including a target-qualified CDecl profile when extraction is available.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
python3 on1x/tools/on1x-mulib.py plan bitset
python3 on1x/tools/on1x-exotract.py list-kernels
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

Compare the plan with the kernel's manifest record and check how inline or external definitions are recorded. A resolved source name can still lack extracted declarations, a native recipe or a callable profile. Capture the target and the qualified C declaration when reporting a mismatch. Check the generated catalogue freshness when changing registered headers. \cref{ch:119} gives a path to navigate the whole manifest.

\section{Decode the bitset plan}
Read the first command's JSON by asking who consumes each field. The C
compiler needs the \OnxCodeTerm{kernels/} directory and the definition selecting the
implementation. The build coordinator needs the count of implementation
translation units. The reproducibility check needs the identities of the
inputs. For bitset, the module row names the canonical header and manual;
the implementation list contains \OnxCodeTerm{MULIB\_IMPL\_BITSET} and the
translation unit count is one. An empty native companion list means there
is no separate companion source to compile for this plan. It does not
mean the bitset implementation can be left out of the executable.

Try reconstructing the plan from just the word ``bitset''. You would
lose the digest of the particular header, the planner inputs, and the
distinction between a requested library and an extra system link library.
For example, blindly converting each name in the requested library list
to a linker option asks the system for a preinstalled bitset library;
that is not how this source plan supplies its definitions.

As a comparison, follow the registration test's discovery plan for QVM.
Its module set contains both QVM and container-core; both headers are
inputs even though neither receives an implementation switch. The
planner can account for a header in the closure without proposing a
standalone archive export. In both examples read the separate safe-export
field before reporting the result. Neither an empty switch list nor a
nonempty one answers a source-language admission question.

For a freshness check, compare the recorded input digest with the
current header bytes. The filename can remain unchanged even when the
old plan no longer identifies the sources you intend to compile.

